A||||5||Smokin87||Bleeding the brakes||||5||
Z||000000||Smokin87||03-21-2001||09:11 AM||||I am replacing the master cylinder, and i have to drain the brakes, and bleed them when im done.  Does anyone know where the relief valve is to drain the brakes, and how do i bleed them when im done.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_copy.jpg">  <BR>"When man wanted to make the car faster...he invented the jet-plane."||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||03-21-2001||11:55 AM||||I don't know of a way do "drain" the brakes. There are bleed screws at each caliper.  You'll use them to bleed the brakes.  As for draining, the only thing I would know if is to purge the system with fluid by bleeding.  Any other people out there with suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||03-21-2001||12:21 PM||||I wouldn't suggest "draining" the fluid out since it's not good to expose the seals to air (this is why they plug the brake line openings to keep air out when you buy new brake components). However when you are done replacing the master cylinder then you should bleed each brake caliper starting with the furthest caliper from the MC. When you bleed the system it's best to flush it until you get clean clear fluid coming out of the caliper.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Smokin87||03-21-2001||02:24 PM||||Thanks, one other question on this, <BR>do i have to remove all the wheels, or is there an easier way, if not its ok, it is just a pain in the @$$.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smokin87||03-21-2001||02:24 PM||||Thanks, one other question on this, <BR>do i have to remove all the wheels, or is there an easier way, if not its ok, it is just a pain in the @$$.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tigger||03-21-2001||02:51 PM||||I picked up a brake bleeder gun from autozone, works great!  Eliminates the need for a friend to pump the brakes (opportunity to save beer & pizza, j/k).  It runs about $30 for the kit, and it's something nice to always have.<P>I'd remove the wheel to get the bleeder screw.||||reg||1
